    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 302258\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n358 PM PDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Generally cooler and drier conditions expected this week, though \nisolated-to-scattered thunderstorms are possible today and Monday in \nour eastern zones.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nThe Four Corners area of high pressure that brought extreme \ntemperatures and isolated showers and thunderstorms to the region \nlast week has shifted eastward thanks to troughing over the western \nUnited States. There remains 0.75 - 1.00 inches of PWAT over the \nregion, with greater values in our eastern zones including Mohave \nCounty and eastern Lincoln County. As a result, with daytime \nheating, there is a 20 percent chance of isolated showers and \nthunderstorms over eastern Mohave County this afternoon, with \ngreater chances further east. Some high-resolution convective \nallowing models continue to show a complex forming over La Pax and \nYavapai counties this evening, with a corresponding outflow boundary \npushing into the Mojave Desert. Should this come to fruition, the \noutflow boundary will bring a moisture flux as well as 250-500 J/kg \nof CAPE along with it, which will return chances of shower and \nthunderstorm development over Mohave, eastern San Bernardino, and \neastern Clark counties tonight in addition to gusty winds. Monday \nafternoon, chances of showers and thunderstorms return, as moisture \nremains over the region and a shortwave pushes into the southern \nGreat Basin. Forecast HRRR soundings show over 2500 J/kg of CAPE \nover Mohave County Monday afternoon, with SPC Excessive Rainfall \nOutlook highlighting the county with a \"Slight\" and areas further \nnorth with a \"Moderate\". Tuesday onward, moisture is scoured out of \nthe region thanks to dry southwest flow aloft. The aforementioned \ntroughing over the region will allow temperatures to drop below-\nnormal for early September with gusty southwesterly afternoons.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Breezy \nsouthwesterly winds will continue into the early evening, with \noccasional gusts to 25 knots likely through sunset. South to \nsouthwest winds will persist overnight, although the gustiness \nshould subside by late evening. Similar wind patterns are expected \nMonday, except the southerly gusts may begin earlier around 16Z. VFR \nconditions will prevail through Monday with any cloud bases \nremaining 12kft AGL or higher. Convection through the remainder of \nthe afternoon will remain well east of the terminal area over \nnorthwest Arizona. Greater convective coverage is expected Monday, \nbut once again the majority should remain east of the terminal area \nand mainly affect approaches from the east. Temperatures should \nremain below 100F through at least midweek. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Outside of the western \nMojave Desert, where winds will favor a more westerly direction \nthrough Monday, southerly to southwesterly winds will continue \nacross the region into this evening. Wind gusts of 20 to 30 knots \nwill be common through sunset, with lighter winds developing \nthereafter. VFR conditions will persist through the TAF period with \nFEW to SCT high clouds AOA 10kft AGL passing across the region. A \nfew isolated thunderstorms will continue over far eastern portions \nof the region into this evening, but no convection is expected to \ndirectly impact area terminals. An increase in convection is likely \nMonday, but once again most of the activity will be over western \nArizona and not directly impact area terminals.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
